= Bancha (番茶)

_Same species as the four Chinese teas (_Camellia sinensis_); a member of the_ Japanese _green-tea tradition, distinguished from Chinese green tea by_ steaming (蒸す mushi) _rather than_ pan-firing (炒る iru) _at the kill-green step — a different national processing lineage on the same plant. Bancha specifically is the_ late-harvest, mature-leaf, every-day _Japanese green tea, the standard household drink and the foundational reference for several derivative preparations (hōjicha, genmaicha, kyobancha, sannen bancha). Status: Japanese — fully part of the Japanese tea tradition documented from Eisai's_ Kissa Yōjōki _(1211 CE) and elaborated in the Edo-period everyday-tea register; bancha as a distinct named drug-form emerges in Edo-period dietetics. Chinese: not canonical (the Bencao tradition treats Chinese 茶; Japanese bancha is a distinct national tradition derived from Tang-Song Chinese tea but developed separately). Ayurveda: not classical. Greco-Roman / European: not classical. Modern Western: substantial reception through the_ macrobiotic _movement (Ohsawa, Kushi) and the wholefoods / health-food tradition, principally via_ three-year bancha (三年番茶 sannen bancha) _and via hōjicha._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Camellia sinensis_ (L.) Kuntze var. _sinensis_ — the small-leaf cultivar that dominates Japanese tea cultivation. The principal Japanese tea cultivar in modern industrial production is *Yabukita* (やぶきた / 藪北), bred at the Shizuoka Tea Experiment Station and released in 1953 — accounting for ~75% of Japanese tea acreage. The drug for bancha is the *mature leaf* of the late harvest (typically the third or fourth flush of the season, _sanbancha_ or _yonbancha_), as distinct from the bud-and-young-leaf material used for the higher-grade *sencha* (煎茶) and the shade-grown *gyokuro* (玉露).#footnote[Species and cultivar identity: standard botanical / agronomic literature; Heiss & Heiss, _The Story of Tea_ (Ten Speed Press, 2007), on the Japanese tea industry; Yamamoto, _The Tea Industry of Japan_, and standard agronomic references for the Yabukita cultivar release (1953). The flush sequence (一番茶 _ichibancha_, 二番茶 _nibancha_, 三番茶 _sanbancha_, 四番茶 _yonbancha_, 秋番茶 _akibancha_) is standard Japanese tea terminology.]

*English:* _Bancha_ — direct transliteration of the Japanese 番茶, via the same Chinese-character / Japanese reading transmission that delivered _matcha_, _sencha_, _gyokuro_, and _hōjicha_ into English. The English word is reasonably well-established in specialty-tea and macrobiotic contexts.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ does carry "bancha" in some editions; the term is widely used in modern English tea-and-wellness literature.]

*Japanese:* 番茶 (_bancha_). The character 番 (_ban_) carries the senses "in order, by turn, common, ordinary, every-day, second-class, late" — the canonical readings include 番号 (_bangō_, "number / serial") and 当番 (_tōban_, "duty turn"); applied to 茶 (_cha_), it gives "every-day tea / common tea / late tea" (with all three valences active). The compound captures the *non-premium, every-day, household* register that defines the drug. Related and adjacent Japanese tea names:
- *煎茶 (sencha)* — the standard non-shade-grown Japanese green tea; steamed, rolled, dried; the dominant Japanese tea by volume; *not* bancha (sencha is from first / second flush).
- *玉露 (gyokuro)* — premium shade-grown Japanese green tea (~3 weeks of shading before harvest); sweet, umami, low-tannin; not bancha.
- *抹茶 (matcha)* — shade-grown, stone-ground powdered green tea; the Japanese tea-ceremony tea; not bancha.
- *焙じ茶 (hōjicha)* — *roasted bancha* (or sencha); roasted over charcoal at high heat to a brown color; the major bancha-derivative; lower caffeine, mellow, comforting; one of the iconic Japanese everyday teas.
- *玄米茶 (genmaicha)* — bancha or sencha mixed with *roasted brown rice* (and sometimes popped rice / 玄米のあられ); the rice contributes nutty notes and "stretches" the tea historically.
- *京番茶 (kyōbancha)* / *Iyobancha* — Kyoto- and Shikoku-region bancha; pan-toasted (some smoked, some sun-dried), with distinctive smoky / woody character.
- *茎茶 (kukicha)* / *棒茶 (bocha)* — *stalk tea*; made from the stems and twigs of the tea plant rather than the leaf; very low caffeine; one of the Western health-food darlings (often confused with bancha but distinct).
- *三年番茶 (sannen bancha)*, "three-year bancha" — a specific macrobiotic / wholefoods bancha *aged for three years* before consumption; particularly low caffeine, "alkaline," and gentle; promoted by the macrobiotic movement.#footnote[Japanese tea-classification terminology: standard Japanese tea-industry literature; Yamamoto, op. cit.; Heiss & Heiss, op. cit. The Kyoto-region kyōbancha tradition with its smoky character is distinctive and is documented in regional Japanese culinary literature. Sannen bancha as a macrobiotic-specific preparation: Ohsawa, _Zen Macrobiotics_; Kushi, _The Macrobiotic Way_; and the broader macrobiotic literature.]

*Chinese:* No classical entry under the name 番茶. Modern Chinese: 番茶 (Mandarin _fānchá_) is occasionally used as a calque for the Japanese category, but is not a classical Chinese drug name; the Chinese category that includes mature-leaf low-grade green tea is simply 粗茶 (cūchá, "coarse tea") or grades thereof.

*Sanskrit:* No classical or modern Sanskrit name.

== Processing and category definition

Bancha is *Japanese-style steam-processed green tea* made from *mature leaves of late-season harvests*; the processing distinguishes it from Chinese green tea at the kill-green step. Canonical sequence:
1. *Pluck* — third or fourth flush of the season (typically July–September), with mature whole leaves and some stems (the stem content varies by grade); the early-flush spring picks have already been used for sencha and gyokuro.
2. *Steam (蒸す mushi)* — *the defining Japanese green-tea step*: leaves are passed through hot steam for 15–60 seconds; this denatures polyphenol oxidase via wet heat. The steaming time defines further sub-classifications (_asamushi_ 浅蒸し, light-steamed; _chūmushi_ 中蒸し, medium; _fukamushi_ 深蒸し, deep-steamed). *Steaming preserves a brighter green color and a fresher, grassier, more vegetal flavor than the pan-firing used in Chinese green tea*, and is the defining national-tradition difference; the chemistry and sensory profile differ accordingly.
3. *Cool and dry first pass* (粗揉 _sojū_).
4. *Roll* (揉捻 _jūnen_) — develops the characteristic needle-or-flake shape.
5. *Final dry* (乾燥 _kansō_).
6. *Sort and grade.*
7. *Roasting* (for hōjicha): bancha is *pan-roasted* or *charcoal-roasted* at high heat (~150–200 °C for several minutes), turning the leaf brown, partially decomposing caffeine and catechins, and producing the iconic toasty-malty hōjicha character.
8. *Aging* (for sannen bancha): the made tea is *stored for three years* (in some traditions, also pre-roasted), allowing slow oxidation and further breakdown of caffeine and astringent polyphenols.

The mature-leaf material has a *very different chemistry* from the young leaf of sencha/gyokuro: substantially *lower caffeine*, *lower L-theanine*, *lower catechins*, *higher fluoride* (mature leaves accumulate more fluoride from soil), and *higher minerals* and *higher polysaccharides*.#footnote[Processing technical detail: Japanese tea-industry literature; the asamushi / fukamushi distinction is industry-standard. The lower-caffeine / lower-catechin profile of mature-leaf bancha relative to sencha is documented in published Japanese tea-chemistry literature (verify against current sources). The roasting-induced caffeine reduction in hōjicha is well-characterized.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_Bancha as a Japanese-tradition tea has no entry in the classical Chinese Bencao tradition; placement on Chinese-medical axes follows the general 茶 framework (see the green tea entry) adjusted for bancha's specific gentler, lower-caffeine, mature-leaf profile. The placement here is reasoned, not classical Chinese attribution._

Reasoned placement: bancha would sit *milder and gentler* on every axis than premium green tea — cooling (涼) to neutral (平) (somewhat *less cold* than premium green tea because of the longer-lived mature leaf and reduced catechins); sweet (甘) with reduced bitter (苦) and astringent notes; Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃), Lung (肺) channels. Expected actions in Chinese vocabulary: 清熱 (clears heat, but mildly), 消食 (disperses food stagnation — the everyday-tea use), 利水 (mild diuretic), 提神 (refreshes spirit — mildly, given the lower caffeine), 和胃 (harmonizes the stomach — distinctive emphasis given the gentler profile and the household-medicinal role). For *hōjicha* specifically, the warming character of roasting shifts the profile toward neutral-warm (温平), with stronger 和胃 / 消食 emphasis.#footnote[Reasoned cross-system placement; the gentler profile of mature-leaf bancha relative to premium green tea is the operative point. Modern Chinese tea-dietetic literature occasionally treats Japanese green teas under the broader 日本綠茶 (Japanese green tea) heading, but bancha as a sub-form is not formally distinguished. Explicitly flagged as reasoned.]

== Classical Japanese tea-medicine tradition

This is the substantive tradition for bancha.

*Eisai (栄西), Kissa Yōjōki* (『喫茶養生記』, "Notes on Drinking Tea for Nourishing Life"), *1211 CE* (revised 1214) — *the foundational Japanese tea-as-medicine text*. Eisai, the Zen monk who established the Rinzai school in Japan and brought tea seeds and the matcha tradition from Song-period China, presented the Kissa Yōjōki to the shogun Minamoto no Sanetomo as a remedy for the shogun's hangover. The text frames tea (then primarily what would later be called matcha) as a "medicine for the five viscera," with the heart (心) as the principal beneficiary; it argues that tea's bitter taste nourishes the heart in the five-phase correlation system. The Kissa Yōjōki is the foundational Japanese tea-and-medicine text and is repeatedly cited in the Japanese tea-and-dietetic tradition for centuries.

*Baisao (賣茶翁 / 売茶翁) and the sencha tradition* — in the 17th–18th c., the Zen monk Baisao (1675–1763) championed *brewed leaf tea* (steeped sencha) over the elaborate tea-ceremony matcha; the modern Japanese loose-leaf tea tradition descends from this. Bancha — as the everyday tea of the working class — emerges in the Edo period as the household-economy form of brewed leaf tea, while sencha and gyokuro became the prestige forms.

*Edo-period household-medical use* — bancha (often consumed with salt for digestion, or as the basis of *kuzu-kombu-umeboshi-tamari* "ume-shō bancha" preparation in folk medicine) is documented in Edo-period folk-medical literature as the canonical everyday digestive and rehydration drink.

*The macrobiotic tradition (20th c.)* — *George Ohsawa* (Yukikazu Sakurazawa, 1893–1966), the founder of modern macrobiotics, drew on the Japanese tea tradition and the yin-yang dietary framework of late-Edo / early-Meiji Japanese folk medicine; *Michio Kushi* (1926–2014) and his students brought macrobiotics to North America and Europe from the 1950s. Within the macrobiotic framework: *three-year bancha (sannen bancha)* is the *recommended daily tea*, valued for *low caffeine*, *alkaline pH* (relative to other beverages), and *gentle warming-tonifying character*. *Kukicha* (twig tea), another low-caffeine member of the bancha family, is similarly valorized. The macrobiotic *ume-shō bancha* preparation (bancha + umeboshi salt-plum + shōyu / tamari + grated ginger) is the canonical macrobiotic digestive / cold-remedy / hangover preparation.#footnote[Kissa Yōjōki: standard Japanese tea-history literature; Eisai's text has multiple modern editions and a substantial scholarly literature. Baisao: see Norman Waddell, _The Old Tea Seller: Life and Zen Poetry in 18th Century Kyoto_ (Counterpoint, 2008). Edo-period folk medical use of bancha: standard Japanese folk-medical literature. Macrobiotics: Ohsawa, _Zen Macrobiotics_; Kushi, _The Macrobiotic Way_, _The Book of Macrobiotics_; the macrobiotic literature is substantial and has its own internal coherence and clinical-traditional register. The *ume-shō bancha* preparation is canonical macrobiotic folk medicine.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_No classical Sanskrit entry; reasoned cross-system placement only._

*Reasoned rasa:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet — distinctive for bancha because of the lower polyphenols), Tikta (तिक्त, bitter — mild), Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent — substantially less than sencha or Chinese green tea).

*Reasoned guṇa:* Laghu (लघु, light), Snigdha (स्निग्ध — *slightly more snigdha than other green teas*, because the mature leaf has different polysaccharide content and the steamed-process tea has different mouthfeel from pan-fired tea), Tīkṣṇa (तीक्ष्ण — *reduced* relative to other green teas, the lower caffeine).

*Reasoned vīrya:* Śīta (शीत, cooling) — but *less cooling than premium green tea*; bancha is the *gentlest of the cooling teas*.

*Reasoned vipāka:* Madhura (मधुर, sweet) — *distinctive shift from green tea's kaṭu* — the lower bitterness and the snigdha character carry through digestion.

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* Pacifies Kapha (the kaphahara core property of all teas); *gentler on Vāta* than premium green tea (the lower caffeine and snigdha shift); broadly Pitta-neutral. Of the green teas, bancha is the *most Vāta-tolerant* — a key dravyaguna distinction and the dietary reason for its everyday-tea status.

*Reasoned karma:* _Kaphahara_, _dīpana / pācana_ (notably stronger than the premium green teas for digestive support), _hṛd-rakṣaka_ (cardiotonic, mild), _laghu-pathya_ (wholesome and light for daily use), _mūtrala_ (mild diuretic), _medhya_ (mild — the caffeine and L-theanine effect, both reduced in bancha), _ucchvāsa-śuddhi_; in excess, minimal compared to other caffeinated teas. The *daily household tonic* register is the cross-system convergence with the Japanese household-medicinal use.

*Hōjicha-specific modifier:* the roasting introduces _uṣṇa_ (warming) and shifts the vīrya from śīta to *neutral / mildly warming*; the roasted notes (toasty / nutty) introduce a _laghu-bṛṃhaṇa_ (light-nourishing) character. Hōjicha is therefore the *most Vāta-friendly* of the green teas in reasoned dravyaguna terms, and is the closest approximation to a *Vāta-pacifying* tea in the green-tea family.

*Sannen bancha (three-year)-specific modifier:* the three-year aging further reduces the tikta-tīkṣṇa register and the caffeine; the drink approaches *nearly neutral* dravyaguna character, suitable for *very depleted Vāta states* where other teas would be contraindicated. This aligns with the macrobiotic clinical use as a recovery drink and the convalescent register.#footnote[Reasoned dravyaguna placement reflects bancha's gentler profile relative to premium green tea. The hōjicha and sannen bancha modifiers are the dravyaguna restatements of the macrobiotic clinical observations. Flagged interpretive.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional, M = modern/pharmacological)_

- *(F) Japanese / macrobiotic folk:* generally permissive — bancha is the *least restricted* of the teas, suitable for children (in dilute form), elders, convalescents, and pregnant women (with caffeine moderation); the household-tea register.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Caffeine load:* substantially *lower* than other teas — typical brewed bancha has ~10–30 mg caffeine per cup; hōjicha ~5–15 mg; sannen bancha ~5–10 mg. *The principal reason for bancha's everyday and convalescent use.*
  - *Iron-binding:* moderate (less than sencha or Chinese green tea); separate from iron-rich meals by 1 hour for iron-deficient patients.
  - *Fluoride:* *the principal modern safety concern specific to bancha*. Mature leaves accumulate substantially more fluoride than young leaves — bancha can contain *2–5 times the fluoride* of sencha. Heavy daily consumption of fluoride-rich tea (relevant especially in the context of fluoridated water + bancha + brick tea) has been associated with dental and skeletal fluorosis in heavy long-term consumers. This is a real consideration for *high-volume daily bancha drinkers*, particularly children. Hōjicha (roasted) may have slightly altered fluoride profile but bulk fluoride remains.
  - *Pregnancy:* the low caffeine of bancha makes it generally pregnancy-friendly within standard caffeine guidance; fluoride load should be reasonable.
  - *Drug interactions:* the standard green-tea EGCG drug interactions are *attenuated* in bancha because of the lower catechin content; less concern than with sencha or matcha.#footnote[Fluoride content of mature-leaf bancha: published Japanese and international tea-chemistry / public-health literature; the accumulation is well-documented and the heavy-consumption fluorosis concern is real, though typically requiring substantial daily intake. Pregnancy caffeine guidance: ACOG, NICE, EFSA.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

Bancha is *not classical in Tibb*. It is a Japanese-tradition late-harvest steamed green tea, exclusively East Asian in pre-modern use and entirely outside the medieval Arabic-Persian medical world. See _green-tea.typ_ for the broader tea-and-Tibb framework. By extension on the green-tea axis, bancha would be classified in modern Persian-Unani as a *milder cooling-and-drying* tea than premium green tea — Cool and Dry in the 1st degree, with a *gentler* digestive register. The macrobiotic *three-year bancha (sannen bancha)* and the *ume-shō bancha* preparation are post-classical 20th-c. developments with no Tibb-tradition lineage. No Avicennan attestation; modern Arabic / Urdu commercial usage is the transliteration *bāncha* (بانچا). For the substantive tea-and-Tibb framework, see green-tea.typ.

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

No classical Chinese formulas use bancha specifically.

=== Ayurveda

No classical Ayurvedic formulas. Modern Indian / Japanese-influenced functional-food use only.

=== Japanese tradition (in place of Greco-Roman section)

This is bancha's substantive tradition.

- *Standard brewed bancha* — the everyday family drink: a generous quantity of leaves (~3–5 g) per pot, with hot (not boiling) water (~80 °C is the Japanese guidance), 30–60 second steep; the canonical Japanese household brew.
- *Hōjicha (焙じ茶)* — roasted bancha; brewed at higher temperatures (boiling water often used, in contrast to the cooler-water guidance for premium green tea); the comforting evening / after-meal tea of Japanese households; particularly favored for children and the elderly because of the low caffeine.
- *Genmaicha (玄米茶)* — bancha (or sencha) with roasted brown rice; classical popular tea, "stretching" the tea historically.
- *Ume-shō bancha (梅醤番茶)* — the canonical macrobiotic / Japanese folk medicinal preparation: bancha brewed strong, with a pitted *umeboshi* (salt-plum), a few drops of *shōyu* or *tamari* (Japanese soy sauce), and a small amount of grated fresh *ginger*. Used for cold-pattern dyspepsia, hangover, common cold, low-grade fatigue, and as a daily morning tonic in macrobiotic practice. The *salty-sour-warming-bitter* taste-combination has a clean Ayurvedic-rasaśāstra (taste-as-medicine) reading and the preparation works as a Vāta-pacifying digestive-cum-cold remedy by the same logic that the Indian masala chai does (taste-by-taste rationale).
- *Kuzu-bancha (葛湯番茶)* — bancha thickened with *kuzu* (Japanese arrowroot, _Pueraria lobata_) starch; warming, soothing; used for cold-pattern digestive disturbance and as a children's cold remedy.
- *Genmai-tamari-bancha* — variant with roasted rice and shōyu.
- *Sannen bancha kombu-cha (note: NOT to be confused with the fermented kombucha 紅茶菌)* — Japanese kombu-cha is bancha with kombu seaweed, served as a clear broth-tea — distinct from the post-fermented sour fermented beverage known in the West as "kombucha," which is a Western re-naming of a Russian-origin fermented black-tea-and-mushroom preparation. *The naming clash is genuine.*
- *Tea-bag bancha* — the modern Japanese mass-market form; convenient but lower-grade.#footnote[Japanese household preparations: standard Japanese culinary and folk-medical literature. _Ume-shō bancha_: Ohsawa, _Zen Macrobiotics_, and the macrobiotic literature; also documented in the Japanese folk-medical literature predating macrobiotics. The Japanese _kombu-cha_ vs. Western "kombucha" naming clash is documented in modern beverage literature and is a real point of confusion.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

*Key constituents (bancha):*
- *Caffeine:* ~1–1.5% of dry leaf (less than half of sencha's 2.5–3%); roasted hōjicha further reduced; aged sannen bancha further reduced.
- *Catechins:* ~10–15% of dry leaf (less than sencha's 15–20%, less than gyokuro's even higher fraction); EGCG is the dominant catechin but at lower concentration.
- *L-theanine:* low to modest (less than sencha or gyokuro, which are the L-theanine-rich Japanese teas because of the shading and youth of the leaf).
- *Polysaccharides:* substantial; some immunomodulatory.
- *Vitamins and minerals:* substantial calcium, potassium, magnesium; some vitamin C in good-grade fresh bancha.
- *Fluoride:* substantial (mature-leaf accumulation; ~5–10 ppm in many samples, vs. 1–3 ppm in sencha).
- *Volatile aromatics:* lighter and more vegetal than sencha; hōjicha adds substantial Maillard-roast aromatics (furfural, pyrazines, maltol).
- *Aged-tea modifications (sannen bancha):* polymerized polyphenols, reduced caffeine, somewhat altered amino-acid profile.#footnote[Composition data: Japanese tea-industry analytical literature; verify against current published data. The L-theanine concentration relationship to youth and shading is well-documented; bancha is a *low-L-theanine tea* relative to sencha and gyokuro.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Caffeine:* the principal axis; the substantially lower caffeine of bancha (and especially hōjicha and sannen bancha) is *the defining pharmacological feature* — bancha is the green-tea form *suitable for caffeine-restricted contexts* (pediatric, pregnancy, evening consumption, anxiety / insomnia patients, elderly).
- *Catechin pharmacology:* applies as for green tea but at lower concentration; modest contribution.
- *Antioxidant:* substantial in vitro; in vivo modest.
- *Cardiovascular:* observational data suggest modest cardiovascular benefits from regular Japanese green tea consumption (the Ohsaki Cohort Study and successor Japanese epidemiology); bancha is a major contributor to this in Japanese dietary patterns.
- *Antimicrobial:* in vitro broad-spectrum.
- *Roasted polyphenols (hōjicha):* the Maillard-modified polyphenols have their own pharmacology — pyrazines and furans contribute to the toasty character and to mild relaxant effects (some studies suggest hōjicha has a calming subjective effect distinct from sencha; mechanistically plausible via reduced caffeine + roasted compound action; effect sizes modest).
- *Fluoride consideration:* the principal modern safety point specific to bancha, with population-level implications for very heavy long-term consumers.
- *Macrobiotic-traditional alkaline claim:* the "alkaline" reputation of bancha (especially sannen bancha) in macrobiotic literature reflects the mineral content (calcium, potassium); the systemic blood-pH effect of dietary acid-or-alkaline foods is *minimal* in healthy individuals (the body buffers tightly), and the popular alkaline-diet framing is *not* supported by current physiology — but the bancha-as-gentle-daily-tonic register is reasonable on caffeine and chemistry grounds alone.#footnote[Japanese green tea cardiovascular observational evidence: Ohsaki Cohort Study (Tohoku University) and related Japanese epidemiology. Hōjicha-specific subjective effects: small studies; verify against current Japanese tea-research literature. The "alkaline diet" framing in macrobiotic literature does not survive modern blood-buffering physiology; flagged.]

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:*
- *Brewed bancha:* 3–5 cups/day or substantially more (Japanese households drink it freely throughout the day); well-tolerated; caffeine load very modest.
- *Hōjicha:* similar; especially appropriate for evening consumption.
- *Sannen bancha:* the macrobiotic register suggests 2–4 cups/day as a daily-tonic; well-tolerated.
- *Risk groups (consider):* (a) *very heavy daily consumption* — fluoride load; (b) iron-deficiency anaemia (separate from iron-rich meals); (c) pregnancy (low caffeine load is friendly; high-volume drinkers should still respect total daily caffeine); (d) infants (dilute; small servings).
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Practical reference figures; standard caffeine guidance applies. The fluoride concern is the principal one specific to bancha; the otherwise broadly-permissive profile is real and the basis of the everyday-tea status.]

